# Non-sensitive tunables for the media stack.
#
# Edit this file, not .env. On smaug this file IS the .env: build-the-nas.md §6
# copies it there unchanged, because every value in it is a plain host fact —
# `make up STACK=media` and scripts/render-config.sh do not run on that host
# (README.md says why). Secrets do NOT belong here; see secrets/README.md. This
# stack has none, by decision (#528; docs/security.md § Secrets): Jellyfin's
# admin account is created through its own setup wizard on first run and lives
# in its database, not in an environment variable, so nothing a secrets file
# held could reach the container.

# The user Jellyfin runs as. Not root — the image declares no user and would
# run as root if this were unset, and nothing else in this estate does.
#
# 65534:65534 is nobody:nogroup, and it works here only because the pinned
# image ships /config and /cache world-writable (mode 777, measured — see the
# comment on the service). It has to be able to read MEDIA_PATH below, so if
# the library's permissions are tightened later, this is the pair that has to
# be given access rather than the mount being loosened.
JELLYFIN_UID=65534
JELLYFIN_GID=65534

# The group that owns /dev/dri/renderD128 ON THE HOST — a host fact, not an
# image one, which is why there is no sensible default to guess here. Getting
# it wrong does not fail loudly: Jellyfin falls back to software transcoding
# and one 4K stream saturates the box, which presents as "the film stutters"
# rather than as an error.
#
# Find it on smaug:
#
#   stat -c '%g %G' /dev/dri/renderD128
#
# ADR-0040's reopen condition turns on this working, and build-the-nas.md §6
# is where it gets tested rather than assumed.
#
# Read off smaug on 2026-09-16: `107 render`, and re-read on 2026-09-19 before
# the deploy. That the node exists at all is worth noting — it means the kernel
# bound a driver to the P630 and exposed a render node, which is one step of
# ADR-0040's condition standing up. The step it is NOT is a container using
# it, and that one was proved on 2026-09-19 (build-the-nas.md §6.1): the node
# is listed inside media-jellyfin, `id` there carries group 107, and a forced
# transcode ran through VAAPI and h264_qsv.
RENDER_GID=107

# Jellyfin's own state — the database, users, watch history, resume positions
# and metadata — bind-mounted into the container at /config.
#
# A DIRECTORY on erebor/apps, not a child dataset and not a named volume.
# Not a volume because TrueNAS keeps named volumes on erebor/ix-apps, and
# build-the-nas.md §4 calls erebor/apps the dataset that is backed up; a path
# here is what makes that true and what a ZFS snapshot can name. Not a child
# dataset because the periodic snapshot task on erebor/apps is not recursive,
# and a second dataset would be a second thing to record. It is created
# `0755`, owned by JELLYFIN_UID:JELLYFIN_GID, before the first `up` — a bind
# mount does not inherit the image's 777 the way a fresh volume does.
#
# This is the half of the tier that IS backed up: scripts/backup-nas.sh reads
# it out of the dataset's newest snapshot from the monitoring host (ADR-0045,
# build-the-nas.md §6.2).
JELLYFIN_CONFIG_PATH=/mnt/erebor/apps/jellyfin/config

# The library on erebor, mounted read-only into the container at /media.
#
# This is the dataset build-the-nas.md §4 creates with a 1M record size and
# deliberately does NOT back up — ADR-0008 ruled the library replaceable and
# its loss "annoying rather than catastrophic". Jellyfin's own state, which IS
# backed up, is JELLYFIN_CONFIG_PATH above and not this path.
MEDIA_PATH=/mnt/erebor/media

# The address Jellyfin's 8096 is published on.
#
# 0.0.0.0 on smaug means its one interface on CasaBonita, 10.0.40.30 — the host
# is single-homed, so there is nothing else it could mean, which is the same
# reasoning stacks/sensitive gives for Caddy's BIND_ADDR. Who may actually
# reach it is decided by placement and by one firewall rule, not by this line:
# every television on 40 reaches it natively on the same broadcast domain, a
# Hicks workstation reaches it through the rule in build-the-nas.md §0.5, and
# default deny sees to everything else.
JELLYFIN_BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0

# The address node_exporter's 9100 is published on.
#
# Same reasoning as JELLYFIN_BIND_ADDR above — smaug is single-homed, so 0.0.0.0
# means 10.0.40.30 and there is nothing else it could mean. The two are separate
# keys rather than one shared one because they answer different questions: 8096
# is published FOR this segment, and 9100 is published for one host on another
# one. If those ever need to differ, they differ here rather than in a rewrite.
#
# Who may actually reach 9100 across a segment boundary is one firewall rule —
# `10.0.99.20 → 10.0.40.30:9100`, build-the-nas.md §0.5 — and not this line.
# Everything already on CasaBonita reaches it regardless; docs/security.md
# records that as the residual it is.
NODE_EXPORTER_BIND_ADDR=0.0.0.0
